This is not a library. It is its name.

KERNEL32.DLL was where the machine kept the things it could not do without:
memory, processes, files, threads. You never opened it, never moved it, and
never thought about it at all - except on the day a dialog appeared saying
that something had performed an illegal operation in KERNEL32.DLL, at which
point you learned its name for life.

The dialog had two buttons. Close, which closed the program, and Details,
which showed an address in hexadecimal that helped nobody who was ever shown
it.

On this system the equivalent is a runtime process, and when something in it
fails the failure is told in words, in a status line a person can actually
read - because a failure told only to a log, or only in hexadecimal, is a
failure told to nobody.
